🌿 Sanjeevani Installation Guide
Sanjeevani is a full-stack web application that leverages deep learning to identify medicinal plants using the InceptionV3 model. Follow the steps below to set up and run Sanjeevani on your local machine.


🔧 Prerequisites
Before you begin, ensure you have the following installed:

Node.js (v18 or higher)

npm or yarn

Python 3.8+

MongoDB (local or cloud-based like MongoDB Atlas)

Git

pip

docker

(Optional) virtualenv


## 1. Environment Variables (`.env`)

Create a `.env` file in the backend directory (.env).  
Required variables:

```env
# MongoDB
MONGO_URI=mongodb+srv://<username>:<password>@<cluster-url>/<dbname>?retryWrites=true&w=majority

# AWS S3 (for image uploads)
AWS_REGION=your-aws-region
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your-access-key-id
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your-secret-access-key
AWS_BUCKET_NAME=your-s3-bucket-name

# Session and Auth
SESSION_SECRET=your-session-secret
GEMINI_API_KEY = gemini_api_key
MODEL_PLANT_API = ABC_live
MODEL_PLANT_HOST = http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict

GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID = google_gmap_client_id
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET = google_gmap_secret_key

# (Optional) CORS
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# (Optional) Server Port
PORT=5000
```

Note:  
- Replace values with your actual credentials.
- If you use Google OAuth, add the required Google client secrets as well.

---

## 3. Install Dependencies

### Backend

```sh
cd backend
npm install
```

### Frontend

```sh
cd ../frontend
npm install
```

---

## 4. Run the Project

### Development (Separate Terminals)

- Backend:
  ```sh
  cd backend
  npm start
  ```
- Frontend:
  ```sh
  cd frontend
  npm run dev
  ```

The frontend will be available at [http://localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173).

---

## 5. Using Docker (Optional)

Build and run the project using Docker:

```sh
cd model
docker build -t fastapi-tf-cpu .
docker run -p 8000:8000 fastapi-tf-cpu
```

---

## 6. Additional Notes

- Make sure your MongoDB and AWS S3 credentials are correct.
- The backend expects image uploads to be sent as `multipart/form-data` with the field name `photo`.
- Logs are stored in app.log.

---

You're ready to go!  
If you have any issues, check your `.env` file and ensure all services (MongoDB, AWS S3) are accessible.

✅ Testing the Setup
Visit http://localhost:5173 to access the frontend

Upload a plant image

The prediction should appear along with plant details

🛠 Troubleshooting
Ensure MongoDB is running

Check if the Python server is accessible from the backend

Use browser dev tools to track API errors

